      "id": 3,
      "text": "The area that became Smoketown was originally part of East Lampeter Township, established in 1729.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/smoketown_pennsylvania",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Early settlers included Palatine German Johannes Buckwalter, who received a land patent in 1723, helping bring German and Swiss farmers to the area.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/smoketown_pennsylvania",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Quaker and Mennonite settlers between 1730 and 1750 shaped the community with pacifism and mutual aid.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/smoketown_pennsylvania",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "A local legend claims Smoketown was named after “three pipe‑smoking Indian maidens,” but historians dismiss it.",
